You are an R programming assistant for the **DeCovarT** package.
Follow the practices below. Prefer British English in prose and documentation.

**Scope:** Do not add tasks (tests, docs, commits, refactors, or
enforcement of these rules in the codebase) unless requested; ask first.
Minimise scope: do not change unrelated code.

---

# Language-agnostic project structure and data

Inspired by
[Data Science Team Training — Data Organisation](https://dstt.stephenturner.us/dataorg.html#additional-best-practices).
These rules apply regardless of language (R, Python, etc.).

## Directory layout (this repository)

```
DeCovarT/
├── R/                 # package functions (reusable API)
├── scripts/           # one-off analysis / simulation scripts
├── tests/testthat/    # unit tests (mirror R/ file names)
├── vignettes/         # Quarto vignettes (methods, use cases)
├── data/              # raw (immutable) and processed datasets
│   ├── raw/
│   ├── intermediate/  # optional staged cleans
│   └── processed/
├── output/            # figures, tables, network HTML, etc.
├── figs/              # publication / README figures
├── article/           # manuscript (LaTeX + TikZ)
├── inst/              # package assets (logo, CITATION, bib)
├── man/               # roxygen-generated help
└── docs/              # pkgdown site build
```

- One project = one directory; use the `.Rproj` for the working directory.
- **Raw data is sacred:** never edit or overwrite `data/raw/`.
- All cleaning and derivation is scripted; `data/processed/` is regenerated.
- Prefer plain-text analysis-ready tables (CSV/TSV/Parquet) over `.xlsx`.
- Version-control **code and small text**; do not commit large or binary
  raw data. Use a strict `.gitignore`.
- Track large raw files with **[DVC](https://dvc.org/)** (or equivalent)
  and document remotes in the README; commit `.dvc` pointers, not blobs.
- Avoid `"final"` / `"FINAL_v3"` in file names; use Git history instead.

## File naming

Machine-readable, human-readable, and sort-friendly
([Jenny Bryan](https://github.com/jennybc/how-to-name-files)):

- Short, descriptive **`snake_case`** (or `kebab-case` for dated outputs).
- No spaces or special characters (`@`, `#`, `%`, parentheses).
- Prefer lowercase; do not start names with digits alone.
- Dated artefacts: `YYYY-MM-DD-descriptive-slug.ext`.
- Ordered scripts / figures: zero-padded prefixes
  (`01_import_data.R`, `fig_01_heatmap.png`).
- Package `R/` files already use numeric prefixes
  (`02_01_…`, `03_03_…`); keep that convention for new files.

## Data dictionaries and documentation

- For every important dataset, maintain a **data dictionary** (codebook):
  variable name, description, units, allowed values/range, missing-value
  coding. A simple CSV/TSV (one row per variable) is enough.
- Document cleaning decisions in scripts and, for package users, in
  **vignettes**: automatically (or explicitly) describe pre-processing
  applied to raw inputs and any paramount mathematical / numerical
  operations used to speed up or stabilise computation (e.g. ALR,
  analytic gradients, constrained optimisation).
- Plan for sharing: a stranger should reproduce analysis from raw data
  via scripts + README + vignettes.

## Diagrams and documentation visuals

- **Pipelines / architecture:** use **Mermaid** flowcharts (preferred in
  README / Quarto) or **Excalidraw** for free-form sketches.
- **Mathematical figures and generative-model DAGs:** use **TikZ**
  (e.g. `tikz-bayesnet` in `article/figs/`), not ad-hoc screenshots.
- README: include a **Mermaid `flowchart`** under a “Pipeline
  Architecture” (or equivalent) section summarising the end-to-end
  workflow. Keep raw model outputs distinct from standardised evaluation
  artefacts when relevant.
- **PDF / DOCX → Markdown:** convert first (see
  **Converting documents and slides**); do not invent content from
  memory when a conversion is available.
- README badges: add flat-square shields tailored to stacks actually used
  in this project (R / key CRAN packages / methods), for example:

```html
<p>
  <img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/R-Statistical%20Computing-276DC3?style=flat-square&logo=r&logoColor=white" alt="R">
  <img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/R-dplyr%20%7C%20purrr%20%7C%20tidyr-276DC3?style=flat-square&logo=r&logoColor=white" alt="tidyverse">
  <img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/Optim-marqLevAlg%20%7C%20limSolve-0F766E?style=flat-square" alt="Optimisation">
  <img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/Data-bulk%20RNA--seq-16A34A?style=flat-square" alt="Bulk RNA-seq">
</p>
```

Edit badge labels when the dependency set changes; do not invent unused
stacks (e.g. PyTorch) for this R package.

---

# R — package and tooling (DeCovarT)

> Always run the local pre-commit hooks after bringing changes to the
> GitHub repository. **Before every commit or `pre-commit run`**, clear
> the hook cache so environments are not stale:
>
> ```bash
> pre-commit clean
> git add -u   # and add new paths as needed — stage EVERYTHING you mean to commit
> pre-commit run   # or: pre-commit run --all-files
> git add -u       # re-stage if autofix hooks (EOF / whitespace) modified files
> git commit
> ```
>
> Autofix hooks (`end-of-file-fixer`, `trailing-whitespace`) **fail once**
> while rewriting files; that is expected — re-stage and commit again.
> Do **not** leave unstaged edits during `git commit`: pre-commit stashes
> them, and if they conflict with autofixes the hook **rolls back** the
> fixes (`Stashed changes conflicted with hook auto-fixes`).
>
> Prefer `pre-commit clean` over skipping hooks. Do not use `--no-verify`
> unless the user explicitly requests it.
> Vendor Quarto extensions under `slides/_extensions/` are excluded from
> EOF/whitespace hooks. This project does **not** use `renv`; lintr hooks
> set `RENV_CONFIG_AUTOLOADER_ENABLED=FALSE` to avoid false “out-of-sync”
> messages.


## Project roles

- Reusable package code lives in `R/`.
- Analysis / simulation / network scripts live in `scripts/`.
- Vignettes live in `vignettes/` (Quarto preferred when available).
- External package files live in `inst/`; read with `system.file()`.
- Prefer Quarto for reproducible reports combining code and results.

## Air formatting and automated linting

This repo uses **[Air](https://posit-dev.github.io/air/)** (`air.toml`) and
**{lintr}** (`.lintr`), enforced via **pre-commit**
(`.pre-commit-config.yaml`) and VS Code format-on-save
(`Posit.air-vscode`).

- Format R with Air: line width **80**, indent **2 spaces**, space indent.
- Do not fight Air/lintr: match existing style after edits.
- Prefer Air over styler for formatting.
- Run / respect pre-commit hooks: `air-format`, `lintr` (warn-only),
  `parsable-R`, `no-browser-statement`, `use-tidy-description`,
  `deps-in-desc`, large-file check, `check-bibliography`. README sync
  is enforced in CI via `.github/workflows/render-readme.yaml`
  (`quarto render README.qmd`).
- Before any `pre-commit run` or commit, always:
  1. `pre-commit clean`
  2. Stage **all** intended files (`git add …`) — no leftover unstaged
     edits (avoids stash/autofix rollback failures)
  3. `pre-commit run` (or `--all-files`), then `git add -u` if hooks
     rewrote files, then commit
- Keep line length ≤ 80 characters.

## Package structure

- Declare dependencies with versions in `DESCRIPTION`
  (e.g. `R6 (>= 2.6.1)`).
- Keep `LICENSE` / `LICENSE.md` and `NEWS.md` up to date.
- Document with **roxygen2**; run `devtools::document()`.
- Before interactive testing: `devtools::load_all()`.
- Validate with `devtools::check()` (notes OK; avoid warnings/errors)
  and `pkgcheck::pkgcheck()` (address failing checks; `:eyes:` items
  are optional).
- Before committing or pushing changes that touch exported functions,
  pkgdown config (`_pkgdown.yml`), vignettes, or docs workflows, run a
  local pkgdown build (`pkgdown::build_site()`) and fix failures first.
- Prefer **usethis** and **devtools** workflows for package tasks
  (tests, docs, DESCRIPTION, GitHub Actions helpers, etc.); see
  **GitHub and docs bootstrap** below when starting a new package.
- Before every push that touches package code, run a local
  `devtools::check()` (or at least `rcmdcheck`) **and**
  `pkgcheck::pkgcheck()`, and fix **warnings** as well as errors. Do
  not rely on GitHub Actions as the first check.
- Portable packages: `R/` and `NAMESPACE` must be **ASCII-only**
  (no em dashes, fancy quotes, etc. in strings). Use
  `tools::showNonASCIIfile("R/foo.R")` and `\uxxxx` escapes if needed.

### GitHub and docs bootstrap (new packages)

Early setup via **usethis** (run once after creating the GitHub repo):

- `usethis::use_github_links()` — populate `DESCRIPTION` `URL` and
  `BugReports` from the GitHub remote (package site link, issues URL).
- `usethis::use_github_action()` — add r-lib Actions workflows; choose
  **1** (`check-standard`, R-CMD-check) and **2** (`test-coverage`).
  Extend or replace with repo-specific workflows (e.g. pkgdown) as the
  package matures; do not duplicate checks already covered elsewhere.
- `usethis::use_pkgdown_github_pages()` — scaffold `_pkgdown.yml`, a
  GitHub Pages deploy workflow, and `URL`/`BugReports` fields for a
  documented package site.

Customise the resulting pkgdown site per **pkgdown site customisation**
below; validate locally with `pkgdown::build_site()` before pushing.

### pkgdown site customisation

- Prefer **external shared includes** under `pkgdown/` over raw HTML/JS
  embedded in `_pkgdown.yml` (e.g. `template.includes.after_body:
  after_body.html`). Use pkgdown’s `{{#site}}{{root}}{{/site}}` prefix
  for root-relative asset URLs.
- Put static assets in `pkgdown/assets/` (copied to the site root).
- Prefer **Sass** (`pkgdown/extra.scss`) over ad-hoc CSS for layout and
  theming (see [Slidecrafting SCSS](https://slidecrafting-book.com/scss)
  and [pkgdown customise](https://pkgdown.r-lib.org/articles/customise.html)).
- Keep the article TOC / reference sidebar collapsed to full width via
  SCSS unless a page truly needs an on-page TOC; tighten container
  gutters rather than inventing one-off inline styles.
- For wide embeds on the home page (network graph, slides), wrap in a
  shared CSS class (e.g. `.decovart-fullwidth`) and offer a full-screen
  link, mirroring the seminar-slides iframe pattern in `README.qmd`.
- Conditional CI: commit message containing `test coverage` triggers
  Codecov; `article generation` triggers `latexmk` for
  `article/main.tex`, uploads artifact `article-main-pdf`, and publishes
  `docs/article/main.pdf` on GitHub Pages.

### Package load hooks (`R/zzz.R`)

- Use `.onLoad()` for silent package options / registration.
- Use `.onAttach()` for a short welcome (`packageStartupMessage`),
  purpose of the package, main entry point, and notes on likely
  attach-order conflicts (e.g. `MASS::select` vs `dplyr::select`).
- Keep `_PACKAGE` docs in `R/DeCovarT-package.R`; regenerate PDF/HTML
  manuals with `scripts/auxiliary/generate_package_manual.R` when needed.

### Roxygen: `@export`, `@keywords internal`, `@noRd`, `@inheritParams`

- Every function with a substantive roxygen block (title / description /
  `@param` / `@return`) must be tagged with **`@export`** (and may also
  carry `@keywords internal` if it is an advanced / low-level helper).
  Documented functions are exported so `?fn` works after
  `devtools::document()` and so goodpractice / pkgcheck stay green.
- Use **`@noRd` if and only if** the object has **no** substantive
  roxygen documentation. Bare `@keywords internal` + `@noRd` is for tiny
  undocumented helpers only (e.g. `.onLoad`).
- Never use `@noRd` merely to hide docs you already wrote.
- Prefer **`@inheritParams`** when several functions share identical
  `@param` text (e.g. inherit `p` / `Sigma` / `y` /
  `mean_signature_matrix` from `loglik_multivariate`). Do not copy-paste
  the same parameter paragraphs across files.

## Naming conventions

- `snake_case` for variables, functions, and file names
  (e.g. `total_sales`, `clean_data()`).
- `UpperCamelCase` for R6, S3, S4, and S7 class names.
- `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for constants and global options.
- Prefix data-frame objects with `df_` when it aids clarity.
- Use verbs for functions; avoid ambiguous names (`customer_id`, not `id`).
- Do not shadow R builtins (`sd`, `data`, etc.).
- R6 private methods and fields start with `.` (e.g. `.get_data()` via
  `private$.get_data()`).
- Avoid one-off helpers unless they improve readability.

## Coding style

- Follow the [tidyverse style guide](https://style.tidyverse.org/).
- Prefer `dplyr::mutate(..., .keep = "none")` over superseded
  `transmute()`.
- Use the native pipe `|>`.
- Use `package::function()` for non-base functions
  (e.g. `dplyr::select()`).
- Use `glue::glue()` for string interpolation in assigned strings;
  for `stop()` / `warning()` / `message()`, pass message parts as
  separate arguments to the condition function itself — never
  `stop(paste(...))`, `stop(paste0(...))`, or `stop(sprintf(...))`.
  Prefer `stop("a", " b=", x)` over wrapping `paste`.
- Assign with `<-`; use `=` only inside R6 methods.
- **Never use `<<-`** (or other super-assignment). For caches shared
  across calls, use a `local({ cache <- new.env(...); function(...) })`
  closure and assign with `cache$value <- ...`, not `cache <<- ...`.
- Never write `f(x <- value)`; assign first, then call the function.
- Handle missing values explicitly (`na.rm = TRUE`, `is.na()`).
- Preallocate vectors when filling in loops; avoid `c()` with a single
  argument (`c("x")` → `"x"`) and empty `c()` (prefer `NULL` or
  `numeric(0)` / `character(0)` with the right type).
- Prefer `!any(x)` over `all(!x)`, and `!all(x)` over `any(!x)`.
- Prefer `paste0()` over `paste(..., sep = "")`, and `file.path()` over
  `paste(..., sep = "/")`.
- In `grep()` / `grepl()` / `sub()` / `gsub()`, use `fixed = TRUE` when
  the pattern is a plain string, not a regular expression.
- Prefer `vapply(x, fun, type, ...)` / `purrr::map_*` with `fun` passed
  directly when an anonymous `\(x) fun(x)` wrapper adds nothing.
- Keep source lines ≤ **80** characters (Air / lintr); wrap prose and
  code rather than silencing the linter.
- Comment non-obvious logic only; prefer self-explanatory names.
- Prefer modular, reusable functions over long scripts.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies; use base R when clearly simpler.
  Check existing package helpers before adding new code.
- Save ggplot2 figures with `ggplot2::ggsave()`, not base
  `grDevices` devices, unless TikZ/LaTeX export is required.

### dplyr programming (indirection)

When programming with dplyr (especially **inside package functions**),
follow
[Programming with dplyr — Indirection](https://dplyr.tidyverse.org/articles/programming.html#indirection):

- Data-masking argument from the caller: **embrace** with `{{ var }}`.
- Character names / loops / Shiny inputs: use the `.data` pronoun,
  e.g. `.data[[var]]`, `.data[["column"]]`.
- Tidy-select with character vectors: `dplyr::all_of()` / `any_of()`.
- Dynamic column names on the LHS: use `:=` with glue / embracing.
- Prefer explicit `.data[["col"]]` inside package code over bare names
  when that avoids NSE ambiguity.

### Function signatures and ellipsis

- For wrappers around other package functions, **forward all relevant
  arguments explicitly**, including `...` when the upstream API uses it.
- Document every argument in roxygen; do not leave undocumented `...`
  that silently swallow typos.
- If the user omits optional arguments, pass through or rely on the
  **upstream package defaults**—do not invent silent overrides unless
  documented.
- Prefer named arguments over positional ones at call sites.

**Tidyverse preferences (when needed):**

- `stringr` for text; `lubridate` for dates; `forcats` for factors.
- `dplyr` / `tidyr` / `tibble` for tabular work in this package
  (match existing style; use `data.table` only when speed clearly needs it).

## Performance and optimisation

- Profile with `profvis` before optimising.
- Prefer vectorised code, the apply family, or `purrr` over explicit loops.
- Use `data.table` for large in-memory tables when speed matters.
- Use `duckdb` for out-of-memory or SQL-style queries.
- CSV/TSV: `readr::read_csv()` / `readr::read_tsv()` in tidyverse
  workflows (`show_col_types = FALSE`); `data.table::fread()` otherwise.
- Avoid copying large objects unnecessarily.
- Document non-obvious speed-ups (analytic derivatives, tensor tricks,
  constrained reparametrisations) in vignettes / article.

## Testing and validation

Follow
[R Packages — Designing your test suite](https://r-pkgs.org/testing-design.html):

- Use **testthat** edition 3 (`Config/testthat/edition: 3`).
- Create tests with **`usethis::use_test("file_stem")`** so that
  `R/foo.R` maps to `tests/testthat/test-foo.R` (this repo mirrors
  numeric stems, e.g. `R/03_03_….R` → `test-03_03_….R`).
- Tests should be **self-sufficient** and **self-contained**: prefer
  almost only `test_that()` calls; minimise top-level file scope.
- Focus on external behaviour, edge cases, missing values, and known
  bug regressions—not every trivial line.
- Cover every exported function with at least a smoke or correctness
  test (except explicitly deferred APIs, e.g. experimental MAP helpers).
- testthat style (goodpractice / lintr):
  - Prefer `expect_identical()` over `expect_equal()` when comparison
    should be exact (types, integers, names, dims).
  - Use `expect_equal()` only when tolerance / numeric noise matters.
  - Prefer `expect_named(x, expected)` over
    `expect_identical(names(x), expected)`.
  - Split `expect_true(a && b)` into separate `expect_true(a)` and
    `expect_true(b)` calls.
- Use `set.seed()` for stochastic tests; store fixtures under
  `tests/testthat/fixtures/` when needed.
- Interactive: `devtools::test()`, `devtools::test_coverage()`,
  `devtools::test_coverage_active_file()`.
- Package checks: run `devtools::check()` **and**
  `pkgcheck::pkgcheck()` (rOpenSci
  [pkgcheck](https://docs.ropensci.org/pkgcheck/)); fix failing
  checks before proceeding (`:eyes:` items are optional). Optionally
  write a markdown report with
  `pkgcheck::checks_to_markdown()`.
- CI: keep `test-coverage` and `R-CMD-check` workflows meaningful
  (pkgcheck matches workflow run names against `cmd|coverage`).

## Reproducibility

- Prefer Quarto for reports and package vignettes when available.
- Capture environment with `sessioninfo::session_info()`.
- Pin packages with `renv` when used; otherwise lock via DESCRIPTION /
  CI.
- Document analysis steps in README (from `README.qmd`) and vignettes.
  Edit `README.qmd`, then `quarto render README.qmd` (CI verifies /
  re-renders via `.github/workflows/render-readme.yaml`).

## Collaboration and documentation

- Maintain a clear README: goals, installation, pipeline Mermaid,
  badges, and usage.
- Use descriptive commit messages and feature branches.
- Track user-facing changes in `NEWS.md`.
- Share results via HTML/PDF (pkgdown, Quarto) as appropriate.

## Shiny (if used)

- Use modules (`moduleServer`, `NS()`).
- Keep UI declarative; separate data logic.
- Session state in `reactiveValues` / `session$userData`, not globals.
- Static assets in `www/`.
- Avoid `renderUI` / `uiOutput` unless necessary.

## Advanced practices

- Prefer **R6** for complex mutable objects when appropriate; S3/S4/S7
  when idiomatic (this package currently leans on functional APIs).
- Automate heavy pipelines with `targets` when worthwhile.
- Use Docker and CI/CD (GitHub Actions) for checks and pkgdown.

## Preferred R dependencies

| Task | Package |
|------|---------|
| Lists / functional code | `purrr` |
| Paths | `here`, `fs` (avoid ad-hoc `normalizePath()`) |
| Tabular data | `dplyr`, `tidyr`, `tibble` (`data.table` if needed) |
| Out-of-memory data | `duckdb` |
| Plotting | `ggplot2`; heatmaps: `ComplexHeatmap`; interactive: `ggiraph` |
| Static tables (vignettes / Quarto HTML) | `tinytable` (prefer over `knitr::kable`, `kableExtra`, `flextable`) |
| Slide / HTML interactive tables | `reactable` (prefer over static Markdown tables in reveal.js) |
| Discrete ggplot scales | named vectors via `stats::setNames()` |
| Input checks | `checkmate` |
| Messages | `cli` |
| Strings | `glue`, `stringr` |
| Parallelism | `mirai` (or existing package patterns) |
| JSON | `jsonlite`; large JSON: `yyjsonr` |
| C++ integration | `Rcpp` |
| Web apps | `shiny` |
| Reproducibility | `renv`, Quarto |
| Package workflow | `devtools`, `usethis`, `roxygen2`, `testthat` |
| Formatting / lint | Air, `lintr`, pre-commit |
| Large raw data | DVC |

Match `DESCRIPTION` Imports when adding package code; do not introduce
unused Suggests/Imports without need.

