# Bibliography

DeCovarT keeps **two separate citation streams**. Do not mix them.

## A. Literature (Zotero → citation keys → Git)

**Source of truth** for papers / books / theses is the author's
[Zotero library](https://www.zotero.org/bastienchassagnol/library).
Do **not** invent BibTeX entries, citekeys, or DOIs; add the item in
Zotero first, then export. Missing metadata may be completed only via
the deterministic Crossref enrichment CLI below (never by unconstrained
LLM guessing).

### Citation keys: Zotero 8+ native vs Better BibTeX

From **Zotero 8 onward** (this machine runs **Zotero 9**), citation keys
are a first-class field in `zotero.sqlite`
(`fields.fieldName = 'citationKey'`). That is enough for `zotc` and for
stable citekeys when the same formula is set under
Edit → Settings → Cite → Citation Key.

**Better BibTeX (BBT) is optional** once native keys are populated. Keep
BBT only for extras (auto-export on change, rich formulas, Better
BibLaTeX, pull export). Otherwise export with Zotero’s built-in BibTeX
into `inst/REFERENCES.bib` / `article/decovart_library.bib`, then
`python3 scripts/curate_bibliography/check_bibliography.py --clean`. Prefer **native**
`citationKey` over a legacy `better-bibtex.sqlite` table; copy or open
the DB with `immutable=1` when the desktop app holds a lock.

### Zotcurate (`zotc`) — collection curation from documents

Use **[zotcurate](https://github.com/jeetsukumaran/zotcurate)** to bridge
`.bib` / `.qmd` / CSV citation keys and Zotero collections. Prefer **CSV**
for tabular outputs; prefer **YAML** for collection-hierarchy snapshots.

```bash
sudo chown -R "$(whoami):$(whoami)" ~/.local ~/.zotc ~/.venvs   # once if needed
bash scripts/install_zotcurate_home.sh
```

| Path | Role |
|------|------|
| `~/.local/bin/zotc` | Wrapper with `-z ~/Zotero/zotero.sqlite` |
| `~/.zotc/*` | Credentials (chmod 600; never commit) |
| `~/Zotero/zotero.sqlite` | Desktop DB + native citation keys |

Library ID: `7172053`.

**Collection membership (leaf only):** assign **exactly one** collection —
the **most specific leaf** matching the paper (e.g.
`…/3) feature selection: one-vs-all/ii) multivariate approaches`).
Do **not** also attach the parent or higher ancestors. Prefer one
accurate leaf over parent+child duplicates.

**Inputs for missing-citation checks:** `.bib`, `.tex`, `.qmd` (and CSV
for collection sync). Ad-hoc collection exports such as
`IV) build signature matrix.bib` are merge sources only — keep them
gitignored at the repo root; after merging run `--clean`.

**Both directions:**

1. **Zotero → Git:** export → `check_bibliography.py --clean` (article
   copy is the `--clean` upstream when both files exist).
2. **Git/docs → Zotero:** `zotc keys extract` then
   `collection add|create` (dry-run first); new items via Web API /
   desktop → **leaf only** → native citekey → re-export → `--clean`.
3. Software tools → `packages.bib`, not Zotero literature.


| Path | Consumer |
|------|----------|
| `article/decovart_library.bib` | LaTeX manuscript |
| `inst/REFERENCES.bib` | Quarto vignettes (literature half) |

- Exactly these two literature copies (independent, kept in sync).
- After every Zotero export: `python3 scripts/curate_bibliography/check_bibliography.py --clean`
  (allowed fields only; DOI-dedupe; sync both copies).
- Allowed fields (whitelist enforced by `scripts/curate_bibliography/check_bibliography.py`):
  `title`, `author`, `editor`, `year`, `journal`, `booktitle`,
  `publisher`, `howpublished`, `doi`, `volume`, `number`, `pages`,
  `isbn`, `issn`, `edition`, `school`, `institution`, `url`.
  Strip everything else.
- Required fields by type (Rdpack / `utils::bibentry`):
  - `@article` — `title`, `author`, `year`, `journal`
  - `@book` — `title`, `year`, `publisher`, plus `author` **or** `editor`
  - `@incollection` — `title`, `author`, `year`, `booktitle`, `publisher`
  - `@phdthesis` / `@mastersthesis` — `title`, `author`, `year`, `school`
  - `@techreport` — `title`, `author`, `year`, `institution`
- Pre-commit hook `check-bibliography` validates literature hygiene and
  `article/main.tex` citekeys (against literature **and** `packages.bib`).

### Completing missing fields (Crossref + bibtex-tidy)

When BibTeX / Rdpack validation fails because metadata is incomplete:

1. Enrich incomplete entries with the repo CLI (DOI-first, then
   title/author/year Crossref search; merge **missing fields only**;
   never overwrite existing values; never invent DOIs):

```bash
python3 scripts/curate_bibliography/bib_enrich.py \
  inst/REFERENCES.bib --write --backup \
  --min-score 92 --report .cursor/bib-enrichment.json
```

2. Review `.cursor/bib-enrichment.json` and the git diff. Reject records
   marked `ambiguous`. Manually verify any `unresolved` / `partial`
   entries (prefer Zotero correction, then re-export).
3. Normalise formatting with `bibtex-tidy`:

```bash
npx bibtex-tidy@1.14.0 inst/REFERENCES.bib \
  --modify --curly --numeric --sort=key
```

If `bibtex-tidy@latest` is stable on your machine, prefer latest. If it
crashes (e.g. current Node 22 edge cases), pin to `@1.14.0` and/or
upgrade Node.js to the latest LTS release.

4. Sync both literature copies and validate:

```bash
python3 scripts/curate_bibliography/check_bibliography.py --clean
python3 scripts/curate_bibliography/check_bibliography.py
```

Install once (user-level with `uv`, no sudo; keeps tooling in home
directory instead of this repository):

```bash
uv venv ~/.venvs/bib-enrich
uv pip install --python ~/.venvs/bib-enrich/bin/python \
  bibtexparser httpx rapidfuzz typer rich
~/.venvs/bib-enrich/bin/python -c \
  "import bibtexparser, httpx, rapidfuzz, typer, rich; print('OK')"
# then run:
# python3 scripts/curate_bibliography/bib_enrich.py ...
# bibtex-tidy via npx; prefer latest Node.js LTS
```

GitHub issues — DOI bridge:

```markdown
- [ ] [Paper title](https://doi.org/10.xxxx/xxxxx) — `@citekey`
```

## B. Software / tools (`packages.bib` — not Zotero)

R, Bioconductor, and similar **software** citations are generated
automatically into a standalone bibliography — **not** stored in Zotero.

| Path | Consumer |
|------|----------|
| `inst/packages.bib` | Quarto vignettes (software half) |
| `article/packages.bib` | LaTeX (synced copy) |

Regenerate (from repository root):

```bash
Rscript scripts/curate_bibliography/generate_packages_bib.R
```

That script discovers packages used in **`vignettes/`** and **`article/`**
only (CRANpkg macros, `library()` / `pkg::`, plus an explicit software
allowlist for prose-only mentions such as `huge`). It does **not** dump
DESCRIPTION Imports/Suggests or the whole package dependency tree.

## C. Quarto vignette YAML (always two bibliographies)

Every `vignettes/*.qmd` must declare **both** files:

```yaml
bibliography:
  - ../inst/packages.bib
  - ../inst/REFERENCES.bib
link-citations: true
```

Example citations: literature `@wuEstimatingHeterogeneousGene2022`;
software `@R-huge`, `@R-BDgraph`, `@R-base`.

```bash
python3 scripts/curate_bibliography/check_bibliography.py --check-qmd
```

---

# Scientific writing (`article/` and `vignettes/`)

Target venue for the manuscript: **Nature Methods** *Article*
(novel method or tool; full technical description; strong validation;
reproducibility; potential to enable new biology). Apply the same
editorial discipline to package vignettes when they present scientific
claims, methods, or benchmarks—not only to `article/`.

LaTeX: use the Springer Nature journal template already in
`article/` (`sn-jnl` / `sn-nature`). Author support:
<https://www.springernature.com/gp/authors/campaigns/latex-author-support>.

## Nature Methods Article constraints

When drafting or editing the main paper (and when vignettes mirror paper
claims), respect these limits unless the user explicitly overrides them:

| Element | Limit |
|---------|--------|
| Abstract | ≤ **150 words**, **unreferenced** |
| Main text | ~**3,000 words** (up to ~5,000 with editorial discretion); excludes abstract, Methods, references, and figure/table legends |
| Footnotes | **None** in the main article |
| References | ≤ **50** |
| Display items | ≤ **6** figures and/or tables combined |

**Article content expectations**

- Lead with the biological / methodological **problem**, then the
  **contribution**, then evidence—not with marketing language.
- Methods must be complete enough for independent reimplementation
  (or point clearly to package vignettes / code for detail that cannot
  fit the word budget).
- Validation must be **comparative** (baselines, ablation, or controls)
  and include reproducibility artefacts (seeds, software versions,
  data availability).
- Do not invent “potential for new biology”; state concrete enabling
  use cases only when supported by results or clearly marked as outlook.
- Prefer TikZ / vector figures for model DAGs and mathematical panels;
  Mermaid is fine in vignettes/README, not as a substitute for article
  display items unless exported to a journal-ready figure.

**Vignettes**

- Prefer Quarto; British English; precise, cited scientific prose.
- Document pre-processing of inputs and non-obvious numerical / mathematical
  devices (ALR, analytic gradients, constrained optimisers) when they
  matter for understanding or speed.
- Keep vignettes reproducible; avoid duplicating the full Article word
  budget—cross-link the paper and package help pages.
- For **static** tables in vignettes / Quarto HTML, prefer
  [`tinytable`](https://vincentarelbundock.github.io/tinytable/)
  (`tinytable::tt()`) over `knitr::kable()`, `kableExtra`, or
  `flextable`. Embed maths in cells with `$...$` (double backslashes in
  R strings) as in the
  [tinytable math section](https://vincentarelbundock.github.io/tinytable/vignettes/format.html#math);
  set `options(tinytable_html_mathjax = TRUE)` in the setup chunk when
  needed. Keep `reactable` for interactive slide / dashboard tables.

### Quarto Markdown conventions (vignettes and `.qmd`)

Prefer constructs that render cleanly in HTML / pkgdown and stay
readable in source. Follow Quarto
[cross-references](https://quarto.org/docs/authoring/cross-references.html)
for figures, tables, equations, sections, and callouts.

### Package and method names (backticks)

In `.qmd` / `.Rmd` prose, put **package**, **software tool**, and
**named method** labels in inline code spans so they stand out from
ordinary English:

```markdown
`limma`, `edgeR`, `DESeq2`, `BioQC`, `AutoGeneS`, `INDEED`,
`MixSim`, `CARNIVAL`, `CORNETO`, `SCTransform`
```

- Prefer `` `limma` `` over bare Limma / limma in running text.
- Keep citation keys (`@R-limma`) and mathematical notation unchanged.
- Do not backtick generic statistical terms (ANOVA, Gini coefficient,
  Shannon entropy, nested CV) unless they are a software product name.
- Mermaid node labels may keep short plain names for readability.

### Lists (Visual Editor–safe; Quarto basics only)

Use **only** the basic Quarto / Pandoc list forms documented at
[Markdown Basics — Lists](https://quarto.org/docs/authoring/markdown-basics.html#lists):

```markdown
* unordered list
  + sub-item 1
  + sub-item 2
    - sub-sub-item 1

1. ordered list
2. item 2
   - sub-item nested under 2
```

Rules for every `.qmd` / `.Rmd` / `.md` document in this repo:

- **Allowed:** unordered lists (`-`, `*`, `+`) and ordered lists
  (`1.`, `2.`, …), including nesting as above.
- **Forbidden:** Pandoc **example lists** (`(@)`, `(@label)`, or any
  line that Pandoc parses as Example-style). In particular:
  - never write `(@eq-…)`, `(@fig-…)`, `(@sec-…)` as a list marker;
  - never hard-wrap a parenthetical so the next line begins with
    `@label)` (e.g. `(see @sec-foo,` / newline / `  @sec-bar) …`) —
    that is parsed as an example list and blocks Visual Mode
    (“Document contains example lists…”). Keep such cites on one line
    or rephrase as `see @sec-foo and @sec-bar`.
- **Forbidden:** Markdown task / checkbox lists (`- [ ]`, `- [x]`).
- **Do not** use unicode step markers (`- ① …`, `- ② …`) as a
  substitute for ordered lists; use real `1.` / `2.` lists instead.
- Cross-references stay mid-sentence: `see @eq-…`, `see @sec-…`
  (never wrapped as `(@eq-…)`).
- **Warnings / notes:** prefer labelled Quarto callouts with
  cross-references (e.g. `::: {#nte-foo .callout-warning}` … `:::` then
  `@nte-foo`), not raw blockquotes with pasted emoji glyphs. See
  [callout cross-references](https://quarto.org/docs/authoring/cross-references.html#callouts).
- **Emoji / icons:** when an emoji shortcode is needed in Markdown,
  use GitHub-style `:name:` tokens from
  [rxaviers/emoji-cheat-sheet](https://gist.github.com/rxaviers/7360908)
  (e.g. `:warning:`, `:information_source:`), not literal unicode
  emoji characters typed into the source. Enable Quarto emoji support
  in the document YAML when shortcodes must render (`from: markdown+emoji`
  or the project’s established emoji setup).
- Label major headings with `{#sec-…}` and cross-link with `@sec-…`
  within a document; across vignettes use pkgdown HTML links
  (e.g. `[synthetic scenarios](synthetic-scenarios.html#sec-…)`).
- Keep Visual Editor–hostile syntax out of vignettes unless a feature
  (callouts, shortcodes) is intentionally required for the published
  HTML and documented here.

## Scientific voice and self-review (no Consensus required)

When editing `article/` or `vignettes/`, write like a careful methods
reviewer would want to read—and self-check before claiming novelty.

### Anti-slop and wording

- Prefer concrete nouns and verbs: name the estimator, the loss, the
  dataset, the baseline, the metric.
- Avoid AI-flavoured filler: “delve”, “leverage”, “robust framework”,
  “comprehensive suite”, “powerful paradigm”, “in today’s landscape”,
  “it is worth noting that”, “plays a crucial role”, empty “novel”
  / “seamless” / “cutting-edge” without a precise claim.
- Avoid stacked hedges (“may potentially suggest”) and false precision.
- Prefer short sentences; one claim per sentence when arguing.
- Define symbols on first use; keep notation consistent with the package
  and TikZ figures.
- Do not anthropomorphise methods (“the model understands”).
- Citations support load-bearing claims; do not cite for decoration.
  Stay within the Article’s ~50-reference budget by prioritising
  seminal methods, direct baselines, and essential datasets.

### Integrity (literature and claims)

- **Never invent papers, DOIs, quotes, BibTeX entries, or “Consensus”
  hits.** Literature citekeys for `.qmd` must come from the Zotero/BBT
  export (`REFERENCES.bib` / `decovart_library.bib`). Software citekeys
  (`@R-huge`, etc.) come from `packages.bib` via
  `scripts/curate_bibliography/generate_packages_bib.R`. If a literature citation is uncertain
  or missing from Zotero, flag it for the user rather than guessing.
- Label claims clearly: established fact (with citation), result of
  *this* work, limitation, or speculation/outlook.
- Flag load-bearing intro assertions that lack a citation.
- Do not overclaim primacy (“first”, “only”) unless the user confirms
  and a defensible search narrative exists.
- Surface gaps honestly: missing baseline, missing negative control,
  metric not standard in the field—do not paper over them with prose.

### Self-review checklist (use when asked to critique or polish)

Calibrate tone: constructive for a strong core idea; direct if a flaw
undermines conclusions. Prefer **1–4 major** and **2–6 minor** issues—
specific remedies, not padded criticism. Acknowledge strengths when earned.

1. **Summary** — What the text claims to introduce; one-line overall
   impression (specific, not generic praise).
2. **Background claims** — For each load-bearing intro claim: supported /
   partially supported / needs citation / overstated.
3. **Citation hygiene** — Missing *obvious* baselines or method papers
   the user should consider (suggest *search queries* or known
   canonical names; do not fabricate full references). Stay mindful of
   the ≤50 reference cap.
4. **Methods currency** — Is the approach described as current practice
   with acknowledged limitations, or does the text ignore better-known
   alternatives without justification?
5. **Validation** — Are comparisons fair (same genes, same mixtures,
   same metrics)? Are reproducibility details present?
6. **Nature Methods fit** — Word counts, abstract length, display-item
   count, no footnotes; does the narrative match an Article (method +
   validation) rather than a software note alone?
7. **Recommendation** (when reviewing): Accept / Minor revision /
   Major revision / Reject-style verdict for *internal* use only, with
   one justifying sentence tied to the major concerns.

When literature verification is needed and no Consensus account is
available, propose precise search queries (OpenAlex, Google Scholar,
PubMed, Semantic Scholar) for the user to run, and integrate only
results they confirm.

## Scientific review

-Check **statistical analysis**, with <https://www.equator-network.org/wp-content/uploads/2013/03/SAMPL-Guidelines-3-13-13.pdf>
- Check **commit activity** with <https://piechowski.io/post/git-commands-before-reading-code>

---

# Converting documents and slides

Do not invent content when a conversion is available. Use a
**structural converter**, then a **cleanup pass** (YAML, headings,
tables, captions, image paths, alt-text). Do not ask an LLM to read a
whole DOCX/PPTX when Pandoc can parse its structure.

## Slides (PDF / PPTX → Quarto reveal.js)

Rebuild as Quarto reveal.js with Slidecrafting patterns; do not ship
raw PDF/PPTX as the live deck. When several sources exist:

1. **Markdown** — structure and wording.
2. **PPTX** — Pandoc extracts images, hyperlinks, lists, and OMML
   equations (to LaTeX):

```bash
pandoc talk.pptx --extract-media=slides/images -t markdown -o talk.md
```

3. **PDF** — Stephen Turner’s
   **[pdfdown](https://stephenturner.github.io/pdfdown/)** (local
   browser drop-zone) for text; `pdftoppm` only for figures pdfdown
   misses.

Keep extracted media. Restore equations as `$...$` / `$$...$$` when
Pandoc emitted LaTeX; otherwise transcribe from the slide. Preserve
links as `[text](url)`. Then restyle with the reveal.js skill.

## Documents (DOCX / PDF → `.qmd`)

**DOCX** is structured. Convert with Pandoc, then clean — never hand
the whole file to an LLM first
([Pandoc Markdown](https://quarto.org/docs/authoring/markdown-basics.html)):

```bash
pandoc document.docx \
  --extract-media=images \
  -t markdown \
  -o document.qmd
```

Yields `document.qmd` plus `images/media/…`, referenced as
`![…](images/media/image1.png)`.

Cleanup pass (do **not** rewrite scientific content): Quarto YAML,
heading hierarchy, Word tables, captions, notes, cross-references,
image paths, and alt-text for screenshots or diagrams.

**PDF** (literature, notes, reviews, vignette drafts): pdfdown first,
then the same cleanup pass; `pdftoppm` only for unrecovered figures.

# Quarto reveal.js slides (Slidecrafting)

Whenever the user asks to create, restyle, animate, or present
**Quarto reveal.js** slides (`format: revealjs` in a `.qmd`, or work
under `slides/` / similar deck folders), follow this workflow.

## 1. Ensure the global slidecrafting skills are installed

Expected personal Cursor skills (from
[EmilHvitfeldt/slidecrafting-book.com](https://github.com/EmilHvitfeldt/slidecrafting-book.com)):

- `~/.cursor/skills/quarto-revealjs/SKILL.md` (main router)
- `~/.cursor/skills/quarto-revealjs-fragment/SKILL.md` (optional; custom fragments)
- `~/.cursor/skills/auto-animate-elements/SKILL.md` (optional; auto-animate casts)

**If `quarto-revealjs` is missing**, stop designing slides from scratch
and **suggest installing** the global skill first, e.g.:

```bash
npx skills add emilhvitfeldt/slidecrafting.com --skill quarto-revealjs
# or all three:
npx skills add emilhvitfeldt/slidecrafting.com
```

(Requires Node ≥ 22.20.) Then continue after the skill is present.

## 2. Always use the skill for reveal.js decks

1. **Read** `~/.cursor/skills/quarto-revealjs/SKILL.md` (or the
   `~/.agents/skills/` copy) **before** writing or heavily editing a
   reveal.js deck.
2. Open the matching `references/*.md` file(s) the skill points to
   (theme, layout, code/plots, fragments, auto-animate, presenting).
3. Prefer Slidecrafting conventions over generic Quarto/reveal.js
   improvisation: theme SCSS, columns/absolute layout, fragment and
   auto-animate patterns, speaker notes / PDF export as documented
   there and at <https://slidecrafting-book.com>.
4. Cross-check features against official Quarto reveal.js docs
   (<https://quarto.org/docs/presentations/revealjs/>) **and** the
   Slidecrafting book (<https://slidecrafting-book.com/>) when choosing
   YAML options, extensions, layout, or animation patterns. Prefer
   documented Quarto / Slidecrafting features over ad-hoc HTML/CSS.
5. For custom fragment CSS/JS or multi-slide auto-animate element
   casts, also read `quarto-revealjs-fragment` and/or
   `auto-animate-elements` when those skills are installed.

Do **not** invent ad-hoc slide CSS/layout systems when the skill
already covers the pattern.

### Live editing (editable extension)

For decks under `slides/`, keep the
[quarto-revealjs-editable](https://emilhvitfeldt.github.io/quarto-revealjs-editable/)
extension installed (`quarto add emilhvitfeldt/quarto-revealjs-editable`
from the deck directory). Wire both the filter and the plugin:

```yaml
filters:
  - editable
revealjs-plugins:
  - editable
```

Use `quarto preview` so the Modify / Save UI can write layout tweaks
back to the `.qmd`. Optionally mark elements with `{.editable}` for
immediate editability without entering Modify mode first.

### Converting legacy decks

See **Converting documents and slides**. Rebuild with Slidecrafting
patterns; do not ship raw PDF/PPTX as the live deck.

## 3. Slide design lessons

### Content principles

- One headline claim per slide. Prefer bullet points over full sentences.
- Prefer **Mermaid** or **Excalidraw** for conceptual diagrams; fall
  back to curated PDF/PPTX page images when the original graphic is
  essential and hard to redraw.
- For tables in slides, use **reactable** (interactive HTML), not static
  Markdown / knitr / tinytable tables, unless the user asks otherwise.
  Keep tables small enough for the viewport (`pagination`, compact
  columns). For **vignette** static tables, prefer `tinytable` instead
  (see Preferred R dependencies and Tables: tinytable vs reactable).
- For equations, always define indexes and other notation on the slide
  (or on the previous slide).

### High-quality scientific images (Nature Methods target)

- Aim for **Nature Methods** visual style: clear scientific message,
  minimal decorative effects, high contrast, and consistent typography.
- Prefer equations and mathematically precise notation when concepts are
  model-based; export equation-heavy diagrams as vector assets (SVG/PDF)
  whenever possible.
- Use multi-panel layouts (`A`, `B`, `C`, …) when several concepts are
  needed to explain one claim; each panel should answer one precise
  question.
- Keep style minimalist and interpretation-first: avoid visual clutter,
  3D effects, and ornamental graphics that do not support inference.
- When creating or revising generated illustrations in `slides/index.qmd`
  and included section files, apply this same panel and readability
  discipline.

### Mermaid rendering constraints (README, vignettes, slides)

- Do **not** use plain code fences like ```` ``` mermaid ```` for figures
  intended to render in Quarto outputs; use executable Mermaid chunks.
  Minimal pattern:
  - opening fence: ```` ```{mermaid} ````
  - options: `%%| label: fig-example` and
    `%%| fig-cap: "Example caption."`
  - diagram body: e.g. `flowchart TD` then `A --> B`
  - closing fence: ```` ``` ````

- Cross-reference Mermaid figures with Quarto labels (`@fig-...`), as
  documented at
  <https://quarto.org/docs/authoring/diagrams.html#cross-references>.
- Set Mermaid theme to **sandstone** for consistency unless a slide
  context explicitly requires another documented theme.
- For reveal.js decks, keep Mermaid configured under `format: revealjs:`
  and verify rendering in both `quarto preview` and `quarto render`.
- For PDF/Word paths using Mermaid, use the installed
  `chrome-headless-shell` backend and verify rendered output rather than
  raw diagram source.

### Citations and cross-references

- Cite from `inst/REFERENCES.bib` (or a deck-local `.bib` that mirrors
  those keys). Wire the deck YAML with e.g.
  `bibliography: ../inst/REFERENCES.bib` and use Quarto cite syntax
  (`@key`, `[@key]`).
- Label figures, tables, and equations for Quarto cross-references
  (`#fig-…`, `#tbl-…`, `#eq-…`) and refer to them with `@fig-…` /
  `@tbl-…` / `@eq-…`. See
  <https://quarto.org/docs/authoring/cross-references.html>.
- Do not silently drop citations when converting legacy slides.

### Tables: tinytable (static) vs reactable (interactive)

- **Vignettes / static Quarto HTML:** use `tinytable::tt()` by default.
  Do not introduce `knitr::kable()`, `kableExtra`, or `flextable` for
  new static tables unless the user asks. For maths in cells, write
  `$\\kappa_2$`-style strings (double backslashes) and follow
  <https://vincentarelbundock.github.io/tinytable/vignettes/format.html#math>;
  enable `options(tinytable_html_mathjax = TRUE)` in setup when HTML
  needs inline MathJax for table cells.
- **Reveal.js slides / interactive HTML:** prefer `reactable` over
  static Markdown / knitr / tinytable tables unless the user asks
  otherwise. Keep tables small enough for the viewport
  (`pagination`, compact columns).

### Tables with reactable

- Add `reactable` under **Suggests** (with a minimum version) when the
  package uses it in slides / vignettes.
- Prefer compact options suitable for reveal.js, e.g.
  `reactable::reactable(..., pagination = FALSE, defaultPageSize = 8,
  bordered = TRUE, highlight = TRUE, wrap = FALSE)` and a modest
  `style` / `theme` so text stays readable.
- Set `echo: false` for table chunks unless the user wants code shown.

### Overflow is the primary failure mode

Reveal.js slides have a fixed viewport. Content that exceeds it
overflows with little or no render-time warning.

- **Always render after edits** (`quarto render …`) and visually check
  the changed slide. Do not assume content will fit.
- When combining a diagram with content below it, budget vertical space
  explicitly. Tighten margins, padding, and font sizes on the upper
  element to leave room.
- If a slide overflows, cut content or tighten spacing — do not shrink
  fonts below readability.
- Use the deck’s existing CSS classes first.
- Add a new CSS class only when a style is reused across three or more
  slides. Do not churn global SCSS/CSS for one slide.

### Font-size hierarchy

For information-dense slides, keep a consistent hierarchy:

| Element | Size |
|---|---|
| Diagram node labels | 18–20px |
| Card / section headers | 14–16px |
| Supporting detail, bullet text | 12–13px |
| Affiliations, metadata, attributions | 10–11px |

Going below 10px is unreadable from the back of a seminar room. If you
need smaller text to fit, the slide has too much content.

### Backbone diagrams as navigation

When a talk has a unifying programme diagram, show it at the opening,
re-show a compact version at major section transitions, and close with
it.

- On first appearance, build in stages if order matters (fragments /
  auto-animate via Slidecrafting). On re-appearances, show it static.
- Colour-code methods in the diagram and reuse those colours when
  referencing the same methods later.

### Figures and attribution

- Check whether requested assets already exist locally before creating
  new ones. Prefer `slides/images/` with relative paths from the slide
  source.
- Enable the **attribution** reveal.js plugin
  (`quarto-ext/attribution`) in YAML:

```yaml
revealjs-plugins:
  - attribution
```

- Place a short credit on the slide with the vertical right-edge
  attribution block (compact by design — do **not** invent HTML
  footers that duplicate it):

```markdown
::: {.attribution}
@schnellImmune2020 — [doi](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41422-020-0277-x)
:::
```

- Pair attributions with labelled figures when the image is the main
  content:

```markdown
![Immune balance seesaw](images/immune-balance.png){#fig-immune-balance}
```

### Editing guardrails

- Do not change slide count, section structure, or title/abstract
  without explicit instruction.
- Do not silently remove citations or attributions.
- Use British English spelling.
- Preserve the citation style and colour palette.
- Hide executed code on slides unless the user wants it shown
  (`echo: false` / Quarto chunk options).
